from save_result import save

save('C0057','stopped-hazard-divergence-not-necessary','Certain discovery with a finite stopped-hazard sum',
'''For a target discovered at time T=1 deterministically, p_n=P(T=n+1|F_n) equals one at n=0 and zero thereafter. Thus discovery has probability one, while sum_n p_n=1 is finite. This refutes a general necessity claim for the actual stopped target hazard. It does not refute a perimeter-only equivalence specific to critical Boltzmann maps; that would require a model-specific argument.''',
'''Take any probability space with the trivial filtration and T=1. The event {T=1} is certain and all other {T=n+1} events are empty. Taking conditional probabilities gives p_0=1 and p_n=0 for n>=1. Summing proves the assertion. A conditional Borel-Cantelli sufficiency statement cannot supply the converse from this example.''',
['Hazards refer to discovery of one fixed target and are stopped at its discovery'],
'A generic counterexample only. It supplies no critical weight sequence whose map is or is not exhausted.')

save('C0005','truncated-decoration-poisson-mean','Exact truncated expectation and infinite full expectation for exponential decoration growth',
'''For Poisson heights of intensity z*exp(-alpha*h) dh, with z>=0 finite and alpha>0, and independent identically distributed nonnegative decoration marks K(s), the truncated sum S_t^H=sum_(-t<=h_i<=H) K_i(t+h_i), H>=-t, has expectation z*exp(alpha*t)*integral_0^(t+H) exp(-alpha*s)*E K(s) ds. If z>0 and exp(-alpha*s)*E K(s)->c>0, the full sum over h_i>=-t has infinite expectation for each fixed t. With E K(s)=exp(alpha*s), the truncated mean is exactly z*exp(alpha*t)*(t+H).''',
'''For a nonnegative marked-Poisson integral, Tonelli and the Poisson first-moment identity give E S_t^H=z*integral_(-t)^H exp(-alpha*h)*E K(t+h) dh, allowing either side to be infinite. Put s=t+h to obtain the displayed identity with exp(-alpha*s), not exp(+alpha*s). Sending H to infinity and applying monotone convergence gives the full expectation z*exp(alpha*t)*integral_0^infinity exp(-alpha*s)*E K(s) ds. A strictly positive limiting integrand makes this integral diverge. The special exponential mark makes the integrand identically one, proving the exact truncated formula.''',
['A marked Poisson process with the displayed intensity','Nonnegative measurable marks independent of the height process','alpha>0 and H>=-t'],
'Infinite expectation does not imply an infinite realized sum: only finitely many heights exceed -t almost surely, and finite decorations can yield a finite realized total. No DGFF decoration moment asymptotic is proved here.')
